 "backlog": [
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "batch",
   "id": "b039",
   "spec": "Compute a weight for each training molecule from how common its Murcko scaffold is within the training fold: w_i = 1 / sqrt(count of molecules sharing its scaffold). Normalise the weights to mean 1. Pass them as sample_weight to the random forest fit and as the weight argument to lgb.Dataset in base_predict. Leave the kernel members unweighted, because the Tanimoto solve has no natural weight hook. The aim is to reduce the influence of large scaffold families on the trees.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Scaffold-frequency sample weighting"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "optimizer",
   "id": "b043",
   "spec": "Replace the Ridge(alpha=1e-3, positive=True) meta-learner with a non-negative least-absolute-deviation fit. Solve min over w >= 0 of sum |oof @ w - y| using scipy.optimize.linprog, with slack variables for the absolute residuals, and then add an intercept term fixed at zero. Use the L1-optimal weights for the final combination of base_te. Keep the oof matrix and the rest of the stack unchanged. The metric is MAE, so this aligns the meta-learner with the scoring.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "L1-optimal non-negative meta-learner"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "other",
   "id": "b047",
   "spec": "Add one round of self-training at the end of fit_predict. After computing the test predictions in the usual way, form an augmented training set consisting of the real training rows plus the test rows carrying their predicted values as pseudo-labels, with sample weights 1.0 for real rows and 0.3 for pseudo-labelled rows. Refit only the LightGBM and random-forest base learners on this augmented set (pass the weights through sample_weight and LightGBM's weight argument), leaving the kernel member and the meta-learner weights from the first pass unchanged, and recombine with the same meta-learner coefficients to give the final predictions. Guard the whole second pass in a try/except that returns the first-pass predictions on any failure, and keep the extra fit cheap enough that total runtime stays well under ten minutes.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Transductive pseudo-label self-training round"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "optimizer",
   "id": "b051",
   "spec": "The kernel-ridge member currently grid-searches lambda in (0.1, 0.5, 2.0) and RBF weight in (0.5, 1.0, 2.0) using one np.linalg.solve per combination per inner fold. Restructure this so that for each RBF weight and each inner GroupKFold(n_splits=4) split, the combined kernel K = Ta[itr,itr] + wr * Ra[itr,itr] is factorised once with np.linalg.eigh giving eigenvalues s and vectors U, and then every lambda is evaluated in closed form as w_in = U @ ((U.T @ (y_tr - mu)) / (s + lam)). Because one eigendecomposition serves all lambdas, widen the grid to lam in (0.03, 0.1, 0.3, 1.0, 3.0) at roughly the current runtime. Select (lam, wr) by summed absolute validation error as now, then refit on the full training block with np.linalg.solve as before. No other part of the script changes.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Eigendecomposition sweep over a wider kernel-ridge lambda grid"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "other",
   "id": "b055",
   "spec": "Simplify the stacking step. Replace meta = Ridge(alpha=1e-3, positive=True).fit(oof, y) with a constrained average: solve for non-negative weights summing to 1 by minimizing mean absolute error on the out-of-fold matrix oof against y, using scipy.optimize.minimize with method='SLSQP' and bounds [0,1], constraint sum(w)=1, starting from equal weights. Predict as base_te @ w. This removes the intercept and the free scale, which may overfit less with a small number of base learners, and matches the MAE objective used by the score. Remove the Ridge import if it is no longer used.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Non-negative averaging instead of positive Ridge meta-learner"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "schedule",
   "id": "b059",
   "spec": "In the stack's LightGBM member, train 900 rounds with a learning rate that decays from 0.06 to 0.01 along a cosine curve. Implement it with lgb.reset_parameter(learning_rate=lambda i: ...) passed as a callback. Keep the other parameters unchanged.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Cosine-decayed learning rate for LightGBM"
  }
 ],
